"""Three follow-ups: a rename, a camera-backed shot page, and text pages.

The panel and the camera share one framebuffer, and until now the only way to
let the camera have it was to hide every object and not let LVGL flush.  That
works for a plain preview but not for a countdown, which has to sit *over* the
picture.  So the ordering becomes explicit: on a camera-backed page the frame
goes down at the tail of the capture loop and LVGL's badge lands on top of it,
which means the badge is redrawn every frame rather than only when it changes.

The information pages move off the grid.  A grid cell is a button; these pages
are read-only, and their content is mostly ASCII, so a single multi-line label
shows far more and costs no new glyphs.  The Chinese titles stay, so the pages
still look like the rest of the panel.
"""
