"""Give the transmit block a master clock divider.

Everything below the block was confirmed on the board: PLL2 locks, IC7 runs
at 22.58 MHz with its gate open, the block is enabled with the right frame
shape, the codec acknowledges, and the DMA channel accepted its
configuration, started, moved five halfwords and stopped.  Five halfwords is
one FIFO fill, which is what a block that never produces a frame looks like
from the bus side.

The missing piece is MCKDIV, which was left at zero.  The vendor HAL's rule
for this configuration - NODIV clear, no oversampling - is

    MCKDIV = kernel clock / (256 * Fs)

and the vendor BSP lands on the same value for the same rate.  Zero is not a
divider: the block's master clock never runs, a frame is 256 master clocks,
so no frame is ever produced, so the FIFO is never drained and the request
line is never asserted.

The fallback feeds the data register from the CPU.  It costs the duration of
the audio and holds the processor, but it needs no request line and no
channel, so playback survives whatever the remaining DMA question turns out
to be - and the rate it plays at is itself a measurement of the clock.
