#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Give the buzzer a sustained tone, and the interface a policy for it.

The buzzer turned out to be a passive one - two test tones came back an
octave apart - so a square wave on PD3 is a note of whatever frequency the
square wave is.  The open question was only how to keep one sounding while
the interface keeps running.

It cannot be done by toggling the pin in the caller: the eye loop is what
decides that the action is still being held, and there is one core, so a
busy tone would starve the very logic asking for it.  TIM3's update
interrupt toggles the pin instead, at twice the note frequency, and the loop
is left alone.  TIM3 is free: /dev/timer0 is TIM2, /dev/oneshot is TIM5, and
the TIM3 PWM lower half is not enabled in this configuration.

The policy, as asked for: on a second-level page the verdict being held *is*
the action, so it sounds continuously for as long as it is held; everywhere
else - stepping through the menu, entering a page - the tone is one short
note once the action has fired.  Each verdict gets its own pitch.
"""
import sys


def read(path):
  return open(path, encoding="utf-8").read().split("\n")


